f $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13195.json" "file_sha256" "d5effa67a56ffb61b8387daad4b064c72d149fbf05ce843292449da3c8ec6b51"} {"question" "e-13196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13196.json" "file_sha256" "ee5a5afde26d4b315e2aec6bb17c4fb7d8e2722c68154d6b6df98973ca09e863"} {"question" "e-13196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13196.json" "file_sha256" "ee5a5afde26d4b315e2aec6bb17c4fb7d8e2722c68154d6b6df98973ca09e863"} {"question" "e-13197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13197.json" "file_sha256" "1277e1c05159978d62bf19f6c79ee1651938343006476b89096acbc1ed45c60d"} {"question" "e-13197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13197.json" "file_sha256" "1277e1c05159978d62bf19f6c79ee1651938343006476b89096acbc1ed45c60d"} {"question" "e-13198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13198.json" "file_sha256" "09746a22cff45b7b282519faf40ab5bf836b468c2f05d072431b0bdf8c713388"} {"question" "e-13198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/example-13198.json" "file_sha256" "09746a22cff45b7b282519faf40ab5bf836b468c2f05d072431b0bdf8c713388"}] "usage" {"q-1192" [] "q-37255" [] "e-13195" [] "e-13197" [] "e-13196" [] "e-13198" [] "q-1210" []} "basis" 1331 "retractions" [[17592186055155 :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A = {{1}, {2, 3}, {1, 3, 6}}$?\n\n1. ${2, {1}}$\n2. ${{2, 3}}$\n3. ${{-1}, {1, 3, 6}}$"] [17592186054889 :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $T = {1, 2, 3}$."] [17592186054900 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A = {x ∈ Z: - 2 \\le x < 6}$ and $B = {x ∈ N: x + 1 < 6}$?\n\n1. $∅⊆B$\n2. $B⊆A$\n3. $A⊈B$"] [17592186054355 :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}$?\n\n1. $\\{-2, 0, 4 \\}$\n2. $\\{0, 3 \\}$\n3. $\\{-3, 1, 3 \\}$"] [17592186054355 :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $S$. Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\le 2n + 1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4 \\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2 \\le n & \\le 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S$. With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{-2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\{0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- $\\{-3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186054356 :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{a, b, c \\}$."] [17592186054356 :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A$, we list out every combination of elements within $A$.\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/50/Source/Images/7bf948604295a59e82bc1c70608fce52.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}\n$$"] [17592186054357 :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n2. $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n3. $\\mathbb{N}$"] [17592186054357 :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186054358 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}$?\n\n1. $A\\subseteq B$\n2. $B\\subseteq A$\n3. $B\\nsubseteq A$\n4. $B\\supseteq A$"] [17592186054358 :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B$.\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10$, that is, $n\\geq 5$. So, we have\n$A= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}$.\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5$, that is, $n\\geq 3$. So, we have\n$B= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}$.\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B$.\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A$.\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B$, which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186051300 17592186051301 17592186051302 17592186051303 17592186051304 17592186051366 17592186051367 17592186051368 17592186051369 17592186051370 17592186052603 17592186052604 17592186052605 17592186052606 17592186052607] "immutable_fields" [[17592186051299 :answer-field/correct] [17592186051299 :answer-field/id] [17592186051299 :answer-field/key] [17592186051299 :answer-field/type] [17592186051365 :answer-field/correct] [17592186051365 :answer-field/id] [17592186051365 :answer-field/key] [17592186051365 :answer-field/type] [17592186052602 :answer-field/correct] [17592186052602 :answer-field/id] [17592186052602 :answer-field/key] [17592186052602 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
